# Writing a {{stinger.name}} client

A client is the *user* of the {{stinger.name}} interface. It {{ client_roles[:-1]|join(', ') }}{% if client_roles|length > 2 %},{% endif %}{% if client_roles|length > 1 %} and {% endif %}{{ client_roles[-1] }}.
Everything it does goes through [`{{stinger.rust.client_struct_name}}`](crate::client::{{stinger.rust.client_struct_name}}), which needs the `client` feature.

## Discovery comes first

A client cannot be constructed out of thin air, because more than one server instance may
be on the broker and the client has to know which one it is talking to — the instance id
is part of every topic it publishes to and subscribes to.

So the sequence is: start an MQTT client, discover a server instance, then build the
client around that instance.

```ignore
use {{stinger.rust.package_name}}::client::{{stinger.rust.client_struct_name}};
use {{stinger.rust.package_name}}::discovery::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}Discovery;

// 1. Your MQTT client, already connected.
let mut connection = /* ... */;

// 2. Find a server. `get_singleton_service` waits for the first one to show up,
//    which is what you want when exactly one server is expected.
let discovery = {{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}Discovery::new(&mut connection).await?;
let instance = discovery.get_singleton_service().await;

// 3. Build the client for that instance.
let mut client = {{stinger.rust.client_struct_name}}::new(connection.clone(), instance).await;

// 4. Keep the message loop running for as long as the client is in use.
let mut client_for_loop = client.clone();
tokio::spawn(async move { client_for_loop.run_loop().await });
```

Until `run_loop` is spawned, the client is subscribed but not dispatching: responses and
signals queue up instead of arriving.

If several servers may be present, use
[`instances_in_discovery`](crate::discovery::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}Discovery::instances_in_discovery)
to list what is currently known, or
[`subscribe`](crate::discovery::{{stinger.name|UpperCamelCase}}Discovery::subscribe) to be
told as instances come and go, and pick one yourself.
{%- if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}

Discovery reports the interface announcement and nothing else — it does not collect
property values, because that would mean receiving every property of every instance
whether or not you ever read one. A freshly built client therefore knows no property
values yet; it subscribes to each property the first time you ask for it.
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}

## Methods

A method call is a request that waits for a response. Each one is an `async fn` on the
client returning `Result<T, MethodReturnCode>` — `Err` means the server rejected the call,
the payload could not be handled, or the response did not arrive in time.

```ignore
{%- for method_name, method in stinger.methods.items() %}
let {% if method.return_value %}result{% else %}_{% endif %} = client.{{method_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in method.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}).await?;
{%- endfor %}
```

The methods of this interface are:
{% for method_name, method in stinger.methods.items() %}
- **`{{method_name}}`** — `{{method_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in method.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}: {{arg.rust.type}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}) -> Result<{{method.rust.return_value_type}}, MethodReturnCode>`
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.signals|length > 0 %}

## Signals

Signals arrive server → client with no reply. Each one is handed out as a
[`tokio::sync::broadcast`] receiver, so any number of places in your program may listen to
the same signal, and listening is entirely optional.

**Asking for the receiver is what subscribes the client to that signal** — nothing is
subscribed to when the client is constructed, so a signal no part of your program listens
for never reaches you. A broadcast channel does not replay either, so **get the receiver
before the signal is sent**, in practice right after constructing the client. A receiver
that falls too far behind drops the oldest messages rather than blocking the client.

```ignore
{%- for sig_name, sig in stinger.signals.items() %}
let mut {{sig_name|snake_case}}_rx = client.get_{{sig_name|snake_case}}_receiver().await;
tokio::spawn(async move {
    while let Ok(payload) = {{sig_name|snake_case}}_rx.recv().await {
        // handle {{sig_name}}
    }
});
{% endfor -%}
```

The signals of this interface are:
{% for sig_name, sig in stinger.signals.items() %}
- **`{{sig_name}}`** — `get_{{sig_name|snake_case}}_receiver() -> broadcast::Receiver<{{sig.payload.rust.channel_type}}>`
{%- endfor %}

The payload type follows the number of values: `()` for none, the value's own type for
exactly one, and a generated `<Name>SignalPayload` struct for several.
{%- endif %}
{%- if stinger.commands|length > 0 %}

## Commands

A command is a method call with no response — fire-and-forget, client → server. `send_*`
returns as soon as the message is on its way. You never learn whether the server acted on
it, and the server never learns that it was you who sent it. If you need an answer, the
interface should define a method instead.

```ignore
{%- for cmd_name, cmd in stinger.commands.items() %}
client.send_{{cmd_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in cmd.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}).await;
{%- endfor %}
```

The commands of this interface are:
{% for cmd_name, cmd in stinger.commands.items() %}
- **`{{cmd_name}}`** — `send_{{cmd_name|snake_case}}({% for arg in cmd.arg_list %}{{arg.name|snake_case}}: {{arg.rust.type}}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %})`
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
