## Properties

The server owns every property; the client holds a cached copy that `run_loop` keeps
current. That cache starts out empty: **a client subscribes to a property the first time
you ask for it**, not when it is constructed, so a property no part of your program reads
costs nothing on the wire.

- `watch_<property>()` — subscribes and returns a `tokio::sync::watch` receiver of
  `Option<T>`. It holds `None` until the server publishes a value and `Some(value)` from
  then on, and wakes on every change. This is the normal way to observe a property.
- `get_<property>()` — subscribes and waits up to five seconds for a value, returning
  `Result<T, MethodReturnCode>`. `Err(Timeout)` means the server never published one.
- `set_<property>(value)` — *requests* a change and returns a `MethodReturnCode`. The
  server may refuse, and the value that actually takes effect arrives through the watch
  channel, not as a return value.
- `get_<property>_handle()` — the underlying lock, for read-modify-write under a single
  commit rather than clobbering a concurrent change.

{%if config.properties.version_tracking%}Everything but `watch_*` waits for the property's current value, because an update request
has to carry the version the client last saw; each can therefore fail with
`MethodReturnCode::Timeout`.{%else%}Everything but `watch_*` waits for the property's current value, so a read or an update
starts from what the server last published; each can therefore fail with
`MethodReturnCode::Timeout`.{%endif%}

Read-only properties have no setter: the server changes them and clients follow along.

{% set example_prop_name, example_prop = (stinger.properties.items()|list)[0] -%}
```ignore
// Reading once. This subscribes to the property and waits for the server's value.
let current = client.get_{{example_prop_name|snake_case}}().await?;

// Observing it. The channel holds `None` until the first value arrives.
let mut {{example_prop_name|snake_case}}_rx = client.watch_{{example_prop_name|snake_case}}().await;
while {{example_prop_name|snake_case}}_rx.changed().await.is_ok() {
    let updated = {{example_prop_name|snake_case}}_rx.borrow().clone();
}
{%- if not example_prop.read_only %}

// Asking the server to change it. `code` says whether the request was accepted,
// not what the value became — that arrives on the watch channel.
let code = client.set_{{example_prop_name|snake_case}}(new_value).await;
{%- endif %}
```

The properties of this interface are:
{% for prop_name, prop in stinger.properties.items() %}
- **`{{prop_name}}`**: `{{prop.rust.type}}`{% if prop.read_only %} — read-only{% else %} — writable via `set_{{prop_name|snake_case}}`{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}

## Errors

[`MethodReturnCode`](crate::payloads::MethodReturnCode) is the one error type you will see
from method calls and property reads and updates. `Timeout` means no response arrived —
usually a server that went away, or a property whose value was never published{%if config.properties.version_tracking%};
`OutOfSync` means the client's cached property version disagreed with the server's.{%else%}.{%endif%} Logging goes through `tracing`, so install a subscriber to see what the
client is doing. With the `metrics` feature, the client and discovery objects expose
counters for messages received and errors seen.

## A note on lifetime

`{{stinger.rust.client_struct_name}}` is cheap to clone, and clones share the same
subscriptions and cached state — clone it freely across tasks rather than building a second
client. Dropping every clone tears down the subscriptions, so keep at least one alive
alongside the `run_loop` task.
